  change events, a subscription concept ("tasks they care about"), and a delivery mechanism. I'd like to keep the spec
  to the notification subsystem itself and treat whatever task/user substrate it needs as an explicit dependency rather
  than quietly designing all three at once.

  First question, and it's the one that determines everything downstream:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this app has a server. Not a style preference — it decides whether a "notification" can ever reach
  someone who isn't currently looking at the page.

  - [1mBrowser-only (localStorage, no backend)[0m — cheapest by far; you can have this working with no infrastructure. But
    "users" means one person per browser, and a notification can only appear while the tab is open. Rules out email,
    push-when-closed, and any real multi-user "someone else changed your task" story. Moving off this later means
    rewriting the storage and event layers.
  - [1mClient + a real backend[0m — makes multi-user, durable, delivered-while-away notifications possible, and is the only
    version where "tasks [3mthey[0m care about" means other people's edits reach you. Costs a server, a datastore, auth, and
    deployment before the first notification fires. Hard to add later; easy to grow into.
  - [1mBackend-shaped but stubbed[0m — build against a data/event interface with an in-memory or local implementation now,
    real server later. Buys the option without the infrastructure; costs some indirection and you won't actually have
    cross-user delivery until the server exists.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser-only[0m, unless you already know this is a multi-user product. The repo is a blank static
  page; committing to a server, auth, and a datastore in order to ship a notification bell is a large amount of
  infrastructure bought on speculation. If multi-user is genuinely the goal, say so now — that's the one piece that's
  expensive to retrofit.
